\description{
Fits normal-likelihood Bayesian meta-analytic models with a
known working variance-covariance matrix \code{V}. The interface supports
location moderators, BayesTools random-effect formulas using
\link[=random_effect_formula_tags]{random-effect formula structure tags}, and
\code{brma.mv()}-specific known-\code{V} backends.
}
\details{
\code{brma.mv()} represents known sampling dependence through a latent \verb{D + BB'}
decomposition of \code{V} (\code{known_v_parameterization = "latent"}), through an
eigen-rotated likelihood (\code{known_v_parameterization = "whitened"}), or
through an exact native block multivariate-normal likelihood
(\code{known_v_parameterization = "block_mvn"}). The default
\code{known_v_parameterization = "auto"} chooses the whitened backend whenever the
extra diagonal variance is scalar, otherwise chooses block-MVN when the
largest dense covariance block is at or below the configured feasibility
threshold, and falls back to the latent backend for large blocks. The automatic
block-MVN threshold defaults to 128 rows per covariance block and can be
changed with \code{options(RoBMA.known_v_block_mvn_max_block_size = value)}, where
\code{value} is a positive integer or \code{Inf}.
Submitted covariance matrices are validated without projecting negative
eigenvalues to the positive-semidefinite boundary. Exact rank-one dependency
blocks are represented by one latent sampling factor, including when another
backend was requested, so correlations of one and any separate positive
diagonal variance retain their mathematical structure.
Singular positive-semidefinite \code{V} matrices are supported only when
integrated conditional variance is structurally positive on every retained
null direction. This can come from strictly positive heterogeneity or a
supported marginalized random-effect variance. The check is performed after
formulas and priors are compiled. Sampled random effects change the
conditional mean and therefore do not regularize the likelihood covariance.
For the block-MVN backend, a fixed point regularizer must also be large
enough to make each block numerically positive definite at double precision;
ineffective fixed values fail during preflight rather than inside JAGS.
The latent known-\code{V} backend is unavailable for other singular \code{V} matrices;
use \code{"auto"}, \code{"whitened"}, or \code{"block_mvn"}. Unsupported configurations
fail before fitting rather than invoking a degenerate-normal likelihood.
As a convenience for diagonal known-\code{V} inputs, \code{brma.mv()} also accepts
named \code{vi} or \code{sei} arguments when \code{V} is omitted; these are converted to
\code{diag(vi)} or \code{diag(sei^2)}. Do not supply \code{V} together with \code{vi} or \code{sei}.

The \code{random} argument uses BayesTools
\link[=random_effect_formula_tags]{random-effect formula structure tags}.
Plain \code{(expr | group)} syntax creates an unstructured random-coefficient
block, and \code{expr || group} creates a diagonal block. Therefore
\code{~ 1 | study}, \code{~ 1 + x | study}, and nested forms such as
\code{~ 1 | study/effect} are supported. The explicit \code{us()} / \code{un()}, \code{diag()},
and \code{id()} tags use the same coefficient-formula semantics. The structured
\code{cs()} / \code{hcs()}, \code{ar1()} / \code{ar()} / \code{har()}, and \code{car()} tags instead own
their index basis; see the linked tag documentation before specifying factor
slopes or index variables.
BayesTools stores fully explicit canonical random-effect names of the form
\verb{(formula) owner: quantity(arguments)}. RoBMA uses their simplified display
aliases consistently in summaries, plotting, density estimation, and
hypotheses. A sole random intercept drops its redundant argument, so a bare
block is displayed and accepted as \code{sd}, while an explicitly named block is
\code{study: sd}; the unique shorthand \code{sd} is also accepted. A non-intercept
coefficient remains explicit, for example \code{study: sd(x)}. Bare formulas and
unnamed one-entry lists omit a redundant owner; explicitly named one-entry
lists retain it, and missing names in longer lists become \verb{component 1},
\verb{component 2}, and so on. Allocation names include \code{sd_total}, \code{sd_common},
and \code{var_prop(study)}. None of these public names expose backend coordinates.
The optional \code{R} argument supplies known covariance or correlation matrices
across random-effect grouping levels, following \code{metafor::rma.mv()} naming.
\code{R} is separate from the known sampling covariance \code{V}: \code{V} describes
sampling-error dependence between estimates, while \code{R} describes the
latent group-axis covariance of random intercepts. Current \code{R} support is
limited to random-intercept blocks. \code{Rscale} controls how each supplied
\code{R} matrix is scaled before fitting and accepts
\code{"cor"}, \code{"none"}, \code{"cor0"}, \code{"cov0"}, plus metafor-compatible aliases
\code{TRUE}/\code{1} for \code{"cor"}, \code{FALSE}/\code{0} for \code{"none"}, \code{2} for \code{"cor0"},
and \code{3} for \code{"cov0"}.
When \code{random} is used, shared sampled
random-effect blocks enter the conditional mean through BayesTools. With
\code{marginalize_estimate_level = TRUE}, a single one-to-one random-intercept
block is integrated into the likelihood variance; otherwise the likelihood
remains conditional on sampled random effects and uses only the known
sampling covariance \code{V}. With a single top-level random component, \code{scale}
models the row-wise total random-effect SD consumed by that component. With
multiple top-level random components, use a named list of \code{scale} formulas
whose names uniquely match the random components. Component names can be
ordinary text; RoBMA normalizes them internally for JAGS. Each formula
models the row-wise total SD for its component. A single \code{scale} formula is
rejected for multiple top-level random components because the target is
ambiguous.
When \code{scale} is a named list, \code{prior_scale} must either be omitted or use the
same named-list shape with one prior list per scale component, using the
same user-facing names. Post-fit scale predictions for random-formula
models keep these component scales separate rather than collapsing them into
one total \eqn{\tau}.
If \code{marginalize_estimate_level = TRUE}, a single random-intercept block that
maps one-to-one to estimates is compiled as a marginalized variance component
instead of sampled latent coefficients. This preserves estimate-level
heterogeneity semantics while keeping shared higher-level random effects
conditional. For known \code{R}, this automatic marginalization is available only
when BayesTools validates a one-column random-intercept block whose
\verb{Z K Z'} row-space contribution is diagonal and one-to-one with the fitted
rows; RoBMA then adds the BayesTools-prepared row multiplier to the known-\code{V}
extra variance. Other known-\code{R} blocks remain sampled or are rejected by
BayesTools validation. Ambiguous cases with multiple one-to-one blocks are
rejected.

The implementation intentionally omits the \code{weights} and \code{cluster} arguments
from \code{metafor::rma.mv()}. Use \code{random} for multilevel structures.
Estimate-unit \code{log_lik()}, \code{add_loo()}, \code{add_waic()}, \code{residuals()}, and
\code{rstudent()} are available for known-\code{V} models. Bridge-sampling marginal
likelihoods via \code{add_marglik()} are available for fitted single-model
known-\code{V} objects. \code{rstandard()} is available for known-\code{V} models at
supported estimate and marginal conditioning depths.
}
\section{Diagnostic target semantics}{

The main \code{brma.mv()} post-fit methods intentionally use different targets:
\itemize{
\item \code{log_lik(unit = "estimate")}, \code{add_loo()}/\code{loo()},
\code{add_waic()}/\code{waic()}, and \code{rstudent()} use estimate-unit log-score
targets. When known \code{V} induces dependence, these contributions are
Schur-complement conditionals \eqn{p(y_i \mid y_{-i}, \theta)} within
dependency blocks. They are existing-estimate diagnostics, not
independent new-estimate prediction targets.
When known random-effect covariance \code{R} is supplied, sampled random
effects remain conditioned in these estimate-depth targets. The known
\code{R} matrix shapes the posterior and prior for those random effects, but
it is not added again as a marginal \eqn{ZGZ'} covariance term. Supported
known-\code{R} blocks compiled as marginalized instead enter through the
diagonal extra variance term \eqn{\tau^2 r_i} prepared by BayesTools.
\item \code{dfbetas()} and \code{covratio()} use estimate-unit PSIS weights. With
correlated known \code{V}, their interpretation is parameter influence under
conditional estimate deletion, not independent-study deletion.
\item \code{add_marglik()}/\code{bridge_sampler()} uses the full fitted likelihood
corresponding to the selected known-\code{V} backend. It does not use the
estimate-wise conditional target from LOO/WAIC. During bridge evaluation,
sampled Gaussian location random effects are integrated exactly as
\eqn{ZGZ'} while retaining every covariance parameter and prior; fitted
diagonal marginalized blocks remain in the row variance. Selection
likelihoods retain their joint latent parameterization.
\item \code{hatvalues()}, marginal \code{rstandard()}, and \code{vif()} use a marginal
GLS covariance target based on \verb{V + ZGZ'}, where formula random effects
are marginalized through the BayesTools covariance metadata.
\item \code{dffits()} and \code{cooks.distance()} use fixed-location fitted-value
influence targets. Cluster/block LOO is deferred for \code{brma.mv()} because
its deletion target is a joint dependency block, not an independent row.
}
Simple \code{brma.mv()} models with scalar heterogeneity report the usual \code{tau}
summary. Random-formula models instead report component-specific standard
deviation, correlation, and allocation summaries.
\code{summary_heterogeneity(component = ...)} describes component heterogeneity
over the observed design, whereas \code{pooled_heterogeneity(component = ...)}
evaluates absolute component heterogeneity at the average expanded random
design. Both report the fitted heterogeneity scale without fixed known-\code{R}
group covariance multipliers; row-specific scale prediction and covariance
consumers retain the full known \code{R}. General known-\code{V} models do not report
scalar I2/H2.
}

